51.m:
Where the river Puṣpabhadrā is, and likewise the stone Citraśilā,
where the banyan tree Bhadravaṭa is, there his hermitage shone;
there in the future his great life-story would unfold.

Protecting them, he once, desiring to see,
went swiftly to the eastern ocean;
and on the shore of that boundless ocean was a forest; there lived serpents.

54.m:
Having obtained the Lord of gods as a husband by this vow, due to pride,
you will suffer abduction by cowherds upon his disappearance;
he who in former times carried off maidens, that god will become your husband.

55.m:
In the first age, there was a king who expounded the Veda and was firm in vows;
desiring a son, he questioned Brahmā Parameṣṭhin.
Brahmā related this vow to him, and he performed it accordingly.

You yourself are the primal sacrifice, O Viṣṇu;
you are the constituent part of the sacrifice, you alone are the oblation.
You are the victim, the sacrificial priest, the object of sacrifice;
hosts of gods and sages sacrifice to you.

